L = lambda t, est, html: {"title": t, "est": est, "html": html}
Q = lambda q, opts, ans, why, src: {"q": q, "opts": opts, "ans": ans, "why": why, "src": src}

LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Sales Order, field by field", "15", "<p>The Sales Order is the hinge of the entire selling cycle: everything before it was persuasion, everything after it is performance. The customer has said yes; this document records exactly what was promised, at what price, for when — and the whole business plans off it: the warehouse sees a pipeline of picks, procurement sees demand, finance sees revenue in flight, and the delivery and billing modules (Selling 5 and 7) will count themselves down against its rows. Walk the fields with that weight in mind.</p><ul><li><b>Customer</b> — always a customer now, never a lead: commitment requires the full master (Selling 1). If the deal began as a lead quotation, conversion happened at the win (Selling 3, Chapter 2), and the order addresses the newborn customer with its group, territory, currency and price list properly set.</li><li><b>Transaction date vs Delivery Date</b> — two different promises. The transaction date is when the commitment was made; the header <b>Delivery Date</b> is when the goods are owed. The gap between them is your fulfilment runway, and every planning view measures against the delivery side.</li><li><b>Per-row delivery dates</b> — real orders split: shelving Monday, imported scanners in three weeks. Each item row can carry its OWN delivery date, and this — not separate orders, not remarks, not holding everything hostage to the slowest line — is the correct model for staggered fulfilment. The header date then reads as the outer promise; the rows carry the schedule.</li><li><b>The items grid</b> — inherited from the quotation when created from one (Chapter 2): items, quantities, UOMs, the negotiated rates with Price List Rate standing witness (Selling 3), amounts. The row additionally carries the <b>warehouse</b> it will ship from — the first appearance of physical reality in the cycle — and, once submitted, live <b>delivered and billed quantities</b> that Chapter 3 explains.</li><li><b>Taxes, terms, T&amp;C</b> — travelled from the quotation (Selling 3, Chapters 4-5): the VAT template, the payment schedule, the conditions legal saw. Verify rather than re-enter; the whole point of document flow is that the accepted offer IS the recorded order.</li><li><b>Source link</b> — the quotation reference, visible on the order. The chain of custody in one field: this order fulfils that offer.</li></ul><p><b>Draft first, always.</b> As with quotations: build in Draft, check against the six-box discipline (right customer, rates as negotiated, dates realistic, warehouse sane, tax template correct, terms intact), then Submit — because submission, as Chapter 3 shows, is not filing. It changes the state of the business.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — From quotation to commitment", "13", "<p>How an order comes to exist determines whether the deal the customer accepted and the deal you fulfil are the same deal. This chapter is the creation discipline.</p><p><b>The preferred path: Create from the quotation.</b> On the accepted quotation: <b>Create &gt; Sales Order</b>. Items, quantities, UOMs, the negotiated rates including overrides, the tax template, the payment terms, the T&amp;C — all carry across with zero retyping, zero transcription errors. The quotation flips to <b>Ordered</b> (Selling 3, Chapter 6), closing its lifecycle, and the order carries the source link forever. This path is preferred not for convenience but for integrity: the moment a human retypes an accepted offer, the agreed deal and the recorded deal can diverge, and every dispute born of that divergence is self-inflicted.</p><p><b>Partial acceptance.</b> The customer accepted four of the six quoted lines. Create the order from the quotation, then <b>delete the declined rows in Draft</b> before submitting — the order records what was actually accepted; the quotation's full offer remains in history; the source link still tells the story. What NOT to do: a fresh hand-typed order for the four lines (orphans the trail, invites typos), or forcing the customer's decision into an all-or-nothing re-quote when the win is sitting in front of you.</p><p><b>Quantity changes at acceptance.</b> Same principle: adjust quantities in Draft. The quotation offered 100; they committed to 80 — the order says 80. (If they may take the other 20 later, that later purchase is a NEW order when it becomes real; do not book hope as commitment. Selling 8's blanket orders exist for genuinely committed future volume.)</p><p><b>Direct entry — the legitimate minority.</b> Orders without quotations exist honestly: standing repeat orders from established customers, counter trade, phone orders in fast-moving distribution. Direct entry serves them — the full grid filled by hand, rates fetching from the customer's list per Selling 2's machinery. The tell of a process problem is not direct entry existing but direct entry DOMINATING in a business that negotiates: if most orders have no quotation behind them in a deal-driven trade, offers are being made outside the system (Selling 3, Chapter 9, Pattern 5) and the pipeline management above them is fiction.</p><p><b>One order per commitment.</b> Resist both fragmentation (five little orders for one purchase because five people phoned) and agglomeration (appending a new week's purchase onto last week's open order). The order maps one commercial commitment; its identity is what every downstream document, status, and report hangs from.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — What submission changes: reservation & the fulfilment ledger", "14", "<p>Submitting a Sales Order is the moment a promise becomes system state. Two machines start, and a professional can explain both to a sceptical storekeeper.</p><p><b>Machine one: stock reservation.</b> The ordered quantities appear as <b>reserved</b> against each row's warehouse. Reserved is not deducted — deduction is the Delivery Note's act (Selling 5), and nothing has physically moved — but reserved quantities subtract from what everyone else sees as available: projected stock reads actual minus reserved plus incoming. The consequence is coordination without meetings: a second salesperson checking availability sees the truth net of promises; the planner sees demand by date; procurement sees what must be bought to cover commitments. Reservation is the mechanism that stops two reps selling the same last fifty cartons — and it is also why <b>stale open orders are poison</b>: an abandoned order nobody closed keeps its reservation forever, hiding sellable stock from the whole company (Chapter 5's Close exists for exactly this, and Chapter 9 meets the disease in the wild).</p><p><b>Machine two: the fulfilment ledger.</b> From submission, the order tracks per row: quantity ordered, <b>delivered so far</b>, <b>billed so far</b> — as quantities and percentages. Every Delivery Note and every Sales Invoice <b>created FROM the order</b> counts these down automatically; the order always knows what remains owed and what remains unbilled. This is the mechanical reason the create-from discipline matters beyond neatness: a delivery note typed from scratch, however accurate its contents, counts against NOTHING — the order will insist those goods are still owed, the status will never advance, and someone will eventually ship them again (Chapter 9, Pattern 1, the most expensive ticket in this module).</p><p><b>What the ledger feeds.</b> The delivered/billed percentages drive the status word (Chapter 4); the remaining quantities pre-fill the next DN and the next invoice (Selling 5 and 7 inherit exact remainders, never guesses); and across all orders, the order-analysis reporting (Selling 6) reads these same numbers as the company's delivery backlog. One ledger, kept automatically, on the document everyone already looks at.</p><p><b>What submission does NOT do.</b> No stock moves. No revenue books. No money is owed. Those are the Delivery Note's and the Sales Invoice's moments respectively — the timeline from Selling 5 bears repeating until reflexive: <i>order reserves, delivery deducts, invoice books, payment settles</i>. A customer who asks why their stock report has not changed after confirming an order gets that sentence, kindly.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The status language", "13", "<p>An order's status is a compact sentence about what remains owed, computed from the fulfilment ledger — not set by hand. Learn to read it, and to explain what moves it.</p><ul><li><b>To Deliver and Bill</b> — freshly submitted: both obligations outstanding. Every order begins here, and stays here while delivered and billed both sit below their thresholds.</li><li><b>To Bill</b> — delivered (fully, within tolerance), not yet invoiced. Goods gone, money not asked for — a status that should be measured in days, not weeks, because it is literally revenue waiting for someone to press Create (Selling 7 turns this into the billing worklist).</li><li><b>To Deliver</b> — invoiced ahead of delivery: the advance-billing flows (Selling 7, Chapter 1). Money asked for or received; goods still owed. Common in project and import trade; the status keeps the outstanding physical promise visible even after finance is satisfied.</li><li><b>Completed</b> — both counts done. The order retires from every pending list.</li></ul><p><b>How the percentages drive it.</b> The status flips on the delivered and billed percentages crossing completion (with over-delivery tolerance, Chapter 7, letting 100%+ within allowance count as complete). This is why hand-fixing a status is meaningless: the status is arithmetic; fix the arithmetic — usually a missing or mis-created downstream document — and the word fixes itself (Chapter 9's diagnostic spine).</p><p><b>Reading the order list as an operations dashboard.</b> Filter by status and the list becomes worklists: <i>To Deliver and Bill</i> sorted by delivery date is the warehouse's week; <i>To Bill</i> oldest-first is billing's morning; per-customer filters show any account's open commitments in one screen — the view to have open when that customer calls. Add the delivery-date column and overdue-to-promise orders surface without any report being built.</p><p><b>Status vs the docstatus.</b> Distinguish the fulfilment status (this chapter) from the document's own lifecycle: Draft / Submitted / Cancelled. A cancelled order (Chapter 6) leaves the fulfilment conversation entirely; a submitted one speaks the four-word vocabulary above. Support conversations go smoother when you name which axis a customer is asking about — <i>the document stands; the fulfilment is what moved</i>.</p><p><b>Per-customer reading.</b> One filter worth teaching every account owner: the customer's own open orders, all statuses, delivery-date column visible — the single screen to have open when that customer calls. It answers, without a report: what have we promised them, what has shipped, what is invoiced, what is overdue to promise. An account managed from this screen never says <i>let me check and get back to you</i> about its own commitments — and for your 30-branch enterprise accounts, that screen IS the service-level conversation.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Hold & Close: the two manual controls", "13", "<p>The ledger runs itself; two decisions remain human. Both change an order's course without falsifying its history — which is precisely why they exist, and why the destructive alternatives are wrong.</p><p><b>Hold — the pause.</b> Credit trouble surfaced (Selling 1, Chapter 6's block may have been bypassed and now the doubt has grown); stock is uncertain; the customer said wait. <b>Hold</b> freezes fulfilment: the commitment stands, the reservation stands, but the order stops being actionable until released. It is not cancellation (nothing is unwound), not closure (nothing is finalised) — a pause, visible to everyone who reads the order, reversible the moment the blocker clears. The discipline around it: a held order carries a reason and an owner — in this house, a dated next step on whoever must clear it — because an unowned hold is just a slow leak with a label.</p><p><b>Close — the honest early ending.</b> The canonical case, worth knowing verbatim: the customer took 80 of 100 and will take no more. <b>Close</b> ends the order deliberately: the delivered 80 stand in history exactly as they happened; the undelivered 20's <b>reservation is released</b> back to sellable stock; the order leaves every pending-delivery and pending-billing list. Walk the rejected alternatives and their specific harms: <i>cancelling</i> falsifies history (the 80 DID ship — a cancelled order says the commitment never stood); <i>deleting the remaining rows</i> mutilates a submitted document and breaks the ledger arithmetic; <i>delivering 20 to scrap</i> is inventing movement to satisfy a status. Close is the answer, and the storekeeper's gratitude is immediate: the phantom 20 stop haunting the projected stock.</p><p><b>Close as hygiene, not just endings.</b> Chapter 3 named stale open orders poison; Close is the antidote at scale. A monthly sweep — open orders past their delivery dates with no movement in N weeks — gets each one a decision: chase it, hold it with an owner, or close it honestly. The projected-stock figure the whole company trades against is only as true as this sweep is regular. (Your SLOT-scale inventory remediations have met the ghost-reservation disease at its terminal stage; this sweep is the vaccine.)</p><p><b>Reopening.</b> A closed order can be reopened when the customer's appetite genuinely returns for the SAME commitment — the reservation re-engages. If what returns is a new purchase at new terms, it is a new order; reopening old paper to house new deals muddies both.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Amendments & cancellation", "13", "<p>Submitted documents are immutable — that immutability is what makes them trustworthy — so change happens through a formal doorway. This chapter is the doorway, its numbering, and its limits.</p><p><b>Cancel, then Amend.</b> Quantities must change, a row must be added, a rate was agreed differently: <b>Cancel</b> the order, then <b>Amend</b>. The amendment is a new revision carrying the same identity with a suffix — SO-00412 becomes SO-00412-1 — editable as a draft, resubmitted when right. The original remains in history, cancelled but readable; the revision chain (…-1, -2) IS the negotiation-after-commitment record, exactly as quotation amendments were in Selling 3. Never a fresh order pretending the first never existed (orphans the quotation link and the customer's paper trail), never database surgery, and never — the recurring temptation — asking a System Manager to edit a submitted document in place: the moment submitted documents can be quietly edited, no document in the system means anything.</p><p><b>What cancellation does mechanically.</b> Cancelling releases the reservation (the promise is withdrawn, stock returns to sellable) and re-opens the source quotation's claim on reality — an order cancelled without amendment leaves the quotation Ordered but unfulfilled, worth tidying when the deal truly dies (mark the quotation appropriately, close the loop).</p><p><b>The linked-documents constraint.</b> An order with <b>submitted</b> Delivery Notes or Sales Invoices against it cannot simply be cancelled — the system refuses, because cancelling a commitment that has partly HAPPENED would orphan real stock movements and real ledger entries. The lawful sequence runs backwards down the chain: deal with the downstream documents first (cancel an unexecuted DN; for executed reality, Selling 5's returns and Selling 7's credit notes unwind what actually occurred), and only then does the order become cancellable. Chapter 9 meets this as a ticket; the answer is always the same sentence: <i>unwind the children before the parent</i>.</p><p><b>Amend vs Close — choosing the right instrument.</b> The confusion to pre-empt: <b>Amend</b> is for correcting what the commitment IS (wrong quantity agreed, missing line, wrong rate recorded). <b>Close</b> is for ending what remains of a correct commitment (they took 80, done). Amending an order down to 80 because the customer stopped buying rewrites history to match the outcome — the 100 was really promised; Close preserves that truth. Corrections amend; endings close.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Delivery tolerance & scheduling", "13", "<p>Paper meets pallets in this chapter: the allowance that absorbs honest overage, and the date discipline that turns orders into a deliverable plan.</p><p><b>Over-delivery tolerance.</b> Real warehouses ship 102 against an order of 100 — pack rounding, bulk goods, good-faith extras. The <b>Over Delivery/Receipt Allowance %</b> defines how much a delivery may exceed the ordered quantity before the Delivery Note is blocked: set per <b>item</b> (the specific tolerance) with the <b>global</b> setting as fallback (Selling 1, Chapter 7's screen). Allowance 5% on 100 → up to 105 passes; 106 refuses. The policy logic, by item class: <b>zero tolerance for serialized goods</b> — every unit has identity, 100 means exactly 100 serials, and slack is how phones vanish; <b>a few percent for bulk</b> — cement, produce, consumables — because blocking the 101st bag helps nobody and the warehouse will otherwise route around the system (the worst outcome: tolerance decided ad hoc at the loading bay). Set it deliberately, per class, once.</p><p><b>Tolerance and completion.</b> Within allowance, an over-delivered row still completes cleanly — 102/100 within 5% reads as delivered, and the status machinery (Chapter 4) proceeds. Outside allowance, the DN blocks at submission and the conversation happens BEFORE stock moves, which is the entire point.</p><p><b>Scheduling with per-row dates.</b> Chapter 1 introduced row-level delivery dates; here is the operating rhythm they enable. The warehouse's forward view is the order rows grouped by delivery date — this week's picks, next week's, the overdue. An order with three delivery waves is one order, three row-dates, three appearances in the plan; the alternative models (three orders, or remarks) either fragment the commitment or hide the schedule from every view. When dates slip, the row's date is updated through the amendment discipline (Chapter 6) if the promise itself is being renegotiated — or stands as the record of the promise missed, which the overdue view then reports honestly. A plan that only ever shows achievable dates because someone edits the misses away is not a plan; it is a mirror.</p><p><b>Promise-keeping as a metric.</b> With dates kept honest, on-time-delivery-to-promise becomes computable per customer, per item group, per period — the operations metric your enterprise clients (30-branch chains with their own service levels to keep) will ask about in the second year of the relationship. The data exists the day the discipline does.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: the Montaigne order, staged and stress-tested", "16", "<p>Selling 3 closed with Montaigne AH accepting quotation v2. This chapter runs that commitment through everything this module taught — including the two bumps every real enterprise order hits.</p><p><b>Step 1 — Birth (Ch. 2).</b> From quotation v2: <b>Create &gt; Sales Order</b>. Six lines carry over — hardware trio at the negotiated rates (the 4% terminal concession intact, Price List Rate still standing witness), installation, support plan, training — with VAT template and the staged payment terms (50/40/10). Montaigne confirms one change at signature: training moves to a later phase, so the training row's quantity drops in <b>Draft</b> before submission. Submit. Quotation v2 flips to <b>Ordered</b>; the order carries the source link; status: <b>To Deliver and Bill</b>.</p><p><b>Step 2 — The schedule (Ch. 1, 7).</b> Twelve branches in three waves: rows carry per-row delivery dates — wave one (5 branches) in 2 weeks, wave two (4) in 5 weeks, wave three (3) in 8 weeks. Hardware rows ship from the main warehouse; tolerance is zero on terminals and scanners (serialized — Ch. 7's rule) and default on consumables. The warehouse's forward view now shows three dated picks; nobody schedules by email.</p><p><b>Step 3 — Submission's machines (Ch. 3).</b> Reservation: 12 terminals, 12 scanners, 12 printers held against the warehouse — the two other pending POS deals this quarter now see availability NET of Montaigne, and procurement sees the reorder demand by date. The fulfilment ledger opens at 0% delivered, 0% billed.</p><p><b>Step 4 — Advance billing (Ch. 4; Selling 7 ahead).</b> Per the terms, the 50% advance invoice is raised FROM the order before any delivery — the ledger records billed% rising while delivered% sits at zero, and the status reads the invoiced-ahead reality. Money machinery continues in Selling 7; the order meanwhile keeps the physical promise visible.</p><p><b>Step 5 — Bump one: the hold (Ch. 5).</b> Week 3: the advance payment stalls in Montaigne's approval chain. Finance requests a pause before wave two ships. <b>Hold</b> — commitment intact, reservation intact, fulfilment frozen — with an owner and a dated next step (chase the advance, review Friday). Payment lands day 4; hold released; wave two proceeds. Total drama recorded: two clicks and one dated follow-up, instead of a WhatsApp thread nobody can find later.</p><p><b>Step 6 — Bump two: the dropped branch (Ch. 5, 6).</b> Week 7: Montaigne cancels one wave-three branch (lease fell through) — they will take 11 sets, not 12. This is an ENDING of part of a correct commitment, not a correction of a wrong one: when wave three delivers its final 2 sets, the order stands at 11 of 12 delivered — and is <b>Closed</b>. The twelfth set's reservation releases to sellable stock; history shows truthfully that 12 were promised and 11 taken. (Amending down to 11 was the rejected instrument — it would rewrite the promise to match the outcome, Ch. 6's distinction earning its keep.)</p><p><b>Step 7 — Retirement.</b> Waves delivered from the order (Selling 5's discipline, each DN pre-filled with exact remainders), final invoices from the order (Selling 7), ledger at completion for 11 sets, order Closed, quotation chain readable end to end: enquiry → meetings → v1 → v2 → order → three waves → close. Anyone in the firm can reconstruct the entire deal in five minutes, two years from now. That is what the hinge document is FOR.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & support patterns", "14", "<p>The Sales Order scar-tissue chapter — and because the order is the cycle's ledger, its diseases are the most expensive in selling. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — The order says goods are owed; the customer says they arrived.</b> Disease: the Delivery Note was created from scratch instead of FROM the order (Ch. 3) — accurate goods movement, zero ledger effect; the order still counts the quantities as undelivered, and the warehouse may ship them AGAIN. Fix: cancel the orphan DN and recreate it from the order (stock timing permitting), or reconcile deliberately; then retrain the create-from reflex. This is the single most expensive ticket in the module — double shipment is real stock and real money.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — Projected stock says nothing is available, the shelves are full.</b> Disease: ghost reservations — stale open orders never closed (Ch. 3, 5). Every abandoned order holds its stock hostage invisibly. Fix: the monthly sweep — open orders past delivery date, no movement — each one chased, held with an owner, or closed; watch projected stock tell the truth again. (At enterprise scale this disease compounds into the phantom-stock archaeology your team has performed for 50-warehouse clients; the sweep is cheaper.)</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — The system refuses to cancel the order.</b> Not a disease — the linked-documents constraint working (Ch. 6). Submitted DNs or invoices exist against it. Fix: unwind the children before the parent — cancel unexecuted downstream drafts, or route executed reality through returns (Selling 5) and credit notes (Selling 7); then the order cancels lawfully. The ticket to refuse: force-deleting via administrative power, which orphans ledgers.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — The delivery is blocked for 2 units of overage.</b> The tolerance gate working as configured (Ch. 7). Fix is a policy question, not a workaround: if this item class genuinely warrants slack, set its allowance deliberately; if it is serialized, the block just saved you — 102 serials against 100 ordered is a conversation, not a shipment.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — Two orders exist for one purchase; both partly fulfilled.</b> Disease: retyping instead of amending, or two staff booking the same phone call (Ch. 2, 6). Fix: consolidate — close or cancel the junior twin after moving any real fulfilment references, then the one-order-per-commitment rule, taught. Prevention: search the customer's open orders before creating (the same search-before-create reflex as Selling 1 and 2's duplicate diseases — third appearance of the same law).</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — Status is To Deliver though the customer already paid everything.</b> Not a bug: advance billing (Ch. 4) — billed complete, delivery outstanding. The status is keeping the PHYSICAL promise visible precisely because finance is satisfied. The support answer is the timeline sentence: order reserves, delivery deducts, invoice books, payment settles — and this order still owes a deduction.</p><p><b>Pattern 7 — Someone edited a submitted order directly.</b> Disease: administrative override culture (Ch. 6). Whatever it fixed today, it broke the system's word: if submitted documents are editable, none of them is evidence. Fix: revert, amend properly, and close the permission hole — this one is escalated as governance, not settled as support.</p><p><b>The meta-pattern, completed.</b> Selling 1-2: documents are downstream of masters. Selling 3: the pipeline is downstream of discipline. This module closes the trilogy: <b>the order is the single source of fulfilment truth</b> — every downstream document is made FROM it, every status is arithmetic ON it, every plan and every promise-metric reads OFF it. Protect that role and the rest of the cycle mostly runs itself; violate it and every module downstream inherits the lie.</p>"),
]

